-- title:
--   Extension parallel networks, encoded by their families of $o,d$-paths
-- statement:
--   An **extension parallel (EP) network** is a directed network with source $o$ and target $d$ built by the grammar: (i) a single arc $(o,d)$; (ii) two extension parallel networks in parallel (sources identified, targets identified); (iii) a single arc in series with an extension parallel network.
--
--   A congestion game played on such a network sees it only through its set $\mathcal P$ of $o,d$-paths, each path being a set of arcs (resources). Accordingly, a family $\mathcal P$ of finite sets of resources is called an **EP family** if it is generated by:
--
--   1. $\{\{e\}\}$ for a single arc $e$;
--   2. $\mathcal P_1\cup\mathcal P_2$ for EP families $\mathcal P_1,\mathcal P_2$ whose arc sets $\bigcup\mathcal P_1$ and $\bigcup\mathcal P_2$ are disjoint (parallel composition);
--   3. $\{\{e\}\cup p : p\in\mathcal P\}$ for an EP family $\mathcal P$ and an arc $e\notin\bigcup\mathcal P$ (an arc in series).
--
--   This is the strategy set of an extension parallel congestion game, the class of games for which Rosenthal's potential is M-convex on load profiles and for which the paper's mixing bound holds.
--
--   **Formalization Note** Rule 3 adds the arc to every path; whether the arc precedes or follows the network does not change the path family. Every EP network's path family arises this way and every family so generated is the path family of an EP network.
-- source:
--   Kleer, Sampling from the Gibbs Distribution in Congestion Games, arXiv:2105.12982v1, §4, pp.14–15 (definition of extension parallel networks)

import Mathlib

namespace GibbsCongestion

/-- **Extension parallel networks**, encoded by their families of o,d-paths
(Kleer, Sampling from the Gibbs Distribution in Congestion Games, arXiv:2105.12982v1, §4, pp.14–15).

An extension parallel network is (i) a single arc `(o,d)`, (ii) two extension parallel
networks in parallel, or (iii) a single arc in series with an extension parallel network.
`IsEPFamily P` says that the family `P` of arc sets is the set of o,d-paths of such a
network, each path being recorded as its set of arcs.

**Formalization Note** A congestion game sees its network only through the set of o,d-paths
(the common strategy set `P`), so the network is encoded by that set. The constructors mirror
(i)–(iii): `arc e` is the network with the single arc `e`; `parallel` joins two networks with
disjoint arc sets, whose path families are united; `series e` puts the arc `e` (not used by the
network) in series, which adds `e` to every path. Whether the arc is placed before or after the
network does not change the path family. -/
inductive IsEPFamily {E : Type*} [DecidableEq E] : Finset (Finset E) → Prop
  | arc (e : E) : IsEPFamily {{e}}
  | parallel {P₁ P₂ : Finset (Finset E)} : IsEPFamily P₁ → IsEPFamily P₂ →
      Disjoint (P₁.sup id) (P₂.sup id) → IsEPFamily (P₁ ∪ P₂)
  | series {P : Finset (Finset E)} (e : E) : IsEPFamily P → e ∉ P.sup id →
      IsEPFamily (P.image (insert e))

end GibbsCongestion


